@startuml
!include style/ail.puml
title Tutorial 4 · Timing: ending a synthetic Watch subscription
hide footbox
$caller(client, "Program\nStarts and ends a Watch")
$callee(api, "Library\nStores and invokes the handler")
$callee(handler, "Program's handler\nA LabelChanged callback")
note over client, api
  watch_labels begins: program lends the handler.
  Library keeps its own reference
  for the Watch's lifetime.
end note
api -> client : watch_labels returns:\nprogram owns the Watch
note over api, handler
  Notifications may start after registration returns.
  The library may also send none
  throughout the subscription.
end note
api -[dotted]> handler : Invoke the handler\nwith a change number
activate handler
note over client, handler
  Program calls destroy_watch from outside this callback.
  Here, the handler is still running on another thread.
end note
client -> api : destroy_watch begins:\nlibrary takes responsibility for Watch
note over handler
  Handler finishes while
  destroy_watch is running.
end note
deactivate handler
note over client, handler
  destroy_watch returns: this subscription has no handler invocation
  running or able to start. This condition is quiescence.
end note
note over api, handler
  Responsibility moves at entry; quiescence holds at return.
  Watch retains no Card.
  Each Label still has its original borrow limits.
end note
legend bottom
  Read top to bottom. This is one permitted sequence, not a delivery promise.
  Person = program calling the library; rectangles = code that receives calls.
  When the library invokes the handler, the library is caller and the handler is callee.
  Bar = handler is running; dotted arrow = handler starts; solid arrow = ownership moves.
  Notes mark other call boundaries. Vertical gaps show order, not elapsed time.
  Non-normative: C3, C5, C6. Native scheduling is omitted; eventual return is not promised.
endlegend
@enduml
